# The Teacher: Yesterday, Today, and Tomorrow
Tamil title: ஆசிரியர் - நேற்று - இன்று - நாளை

The following is a summary of the special address delivered by Arignar Anna on 15 September 1960 before the Trainee Teachers’ Association of the Nehru Basic Training School at Puthanampatti:

“As the chairperson said, the subject on which I am to speak here today is ‘The Teacher’s Position: Yesterday, Today, and Tomorrow.’ I commend him for choosing a fine topic, one necessary to the country and worthy of examination, and for asking me to speak upon it.

“They have selected this subject out of a desire to determine what tomorrow’s position should be by considering the position of teachers today.

“We understand yesterday’s condition well. It was an age in which anything was readily believed. Yesterday’s condition, when the manner in which something was said could easily make anyone believe it, has changed today. A desire to learn and understand everything has now arisen.

“Today, the longing and eagerness to learn and understand everything has begun to spread fruitfully through the country. The day when superstitions gradually disappear is approaching. You who are to become tomorrow’s teachers must undertake this worthy service.

“But I know well that teachers performing such intellectual work cannot carry out the task they have accepted efficiently unless their pay is increased.

“However widely they may read and acquire knowledge, teachers too cannot escape the rise in the cost of living. Their pay must rise. Higher pay will give them peace of mind and enable them to work with happiness. On this occasion, I wish to say that the government must come forward to provide that assistance.

“Nothing is gained by withholding it and instead saying, ‘Teachers, it is you who create scholars in the country and form scientists and graduates. You even create ministers. Your work is therefore necessary to the nation; it is an exceptionally noble work. Having accepted responsibility for creating tomorrow’s world, you must not involve yourselves in petty problems. Like labourers, you must not ask for this and that.’ Such words may serve as ceremonial compliments, but they cannot give contentment. One will understand only by visiting teachers’ homes.

“Only they know the hardship endured by husband and wife and their struggle with life. A teacher cannot delight his wife by saying, ‘Have you seen how Valluvar arranged the words in the eighty-fourth Kural, and with what richness of meaning?’ She will immediately reply, ‘Let the eighty-fourth Kural wait. There is no oil in the house. What do you say to that?’ The teacher must answer her.

“That is why I say that a mendicant or a padishah may perhaps sit listening to a word or two of ceremonial praise. But teachers need proper pay to perform their profession, no, their service, effectively. It is absolutely necessary. If that need is met, education will flourish in the country, the number of educated people will grow, and the government too will receive the benefit.

“A friend I know in Kanchipuram worked as a professor of English. He has now received an opportunity to become a community-development officer and is serving in that position. Another man who worked as a lecturer in history has become a doctor. Yet another threw aside the teaching profession and went to work as an auditor. Many people are similarly leaving for various fields. What is the reason? This condition continues because there is no proper pay. It must change.

“Teachers must not stop after communicating to others the learning they have acquired. They must study more and more, learn new ideas, and give what they learn to their students. Students will leave when they have completed their course of study. Teachers, besides serving as teachers to students every year, must themselves remain permanent students. For this they need abundant peace of mind, adequate leisure, and a good library.

“Ministers have said in the Legislative Assembly that every year the government allocates an increasing amount of money to education. But I ask: however large the sum spent, the population is larger still, and not only that, the benefit obtained is very small. We must understand that; indeed, it is essential.

“Some study only through the sixth standard and stop midway. Some leave after finishing the tenth standard. Others cross the threshold of a college and return midway. Government money is spent even upon those who discontinue their education after studying only partially. Such expenditure is called a ‘national loss.’ Only when education is brought to completion without such national loss will an environment arise in which everyone in the country receives education, and the purpose of our expenditure be fulfilled.

“Today, whatever subject we examine and whoever we examine, we find more people possessing only half-knowledge. For example, suppose there is a radio. It plays when we turn its knob. Can we claim that merely knowing how to turn the knob gives us complete knowledge of the radio?

“We read in a newspaper that there is unrest in the Congo and conflict between the rulers and political leaders. Ask someone who its prime minister is, who its leader is, or where the Congo lies. Many will not know.

“The school curriculum too has been designed in this fragmented way. Such half-knowledge will not advance national welfare. We need complete and lucid knowledge. Curricula must be changed through new methods accordingly.

“There is still a double knowledge in education, and it places teachers in an embarrassing position. For example, when a Tamil teacher teaches a lesson, even if he himself does not believe it, he must say that the Ganges originates in the matted locks of Lord Siva. The students must listen to that. In the next class, the geography teacher comes and explains that the Ganges originates in the Himalayas. If the students repeat what the Tamil teacher said and ask the geography teacher, ‘Why do you say this instead?’ what can he tell them? Can he say which is correct? Is such double knowledge necessary for students? Is this a suitable path to knowledge? I ask people of goodwill and elders kindly to reflect upon it.

“Therefore, to change this condition, to ensure that students of the future receive clear and sound knowledge, and to make new ideas shine in the minds of future young people, today’s curricula must be redesigned. Only then can teachers impart knowledge to students and work with enthusiasm in that task.

“I recall the interpretation offered by an English teacher when explaining a well-known poem. I think you too have studied it.

“The poem says that a star twinkles like a diamond. The teacher explained that saying a star twinkles like a diamond is not right, because not everyone could have seen a diamond. Could an ordinary poor person have seen one? No. If one wished to explain what a diamond looks like, one might say that it resembles a star. But the comparison as given is not something everyone can perceive and understand.

“For our convenience, did we not replace the system of sixteen annas to a rupee with one hundred naye paise? Did the age of the earthen oil lamp not change and the Petromax lamp burning here arrive? Do electric lights not shine in cities and many other places? Do we reject all this and retain only the earthen lamp and standing oil lamp, thinking, ‘These were arrangements made by our ancestors and must not be transgressed’?

“We accept all the marvels of science introduced by someone who came here from elsewhere. Why then should we not throw far away the poisonous ideas, destructive thoughts, and puranic refuse that have ruined our society for ages, and accept modern ideas, the history of a new world, and sound scientific knowledge?

“The wealth of education is imperishable with time. In the country, that wealth must not remain like bars of gold beneath rock in a mine. It must be brought out, put to use, and made into shining gold filled with fine light.

“Teachers and trainee friends, you must work energetically toward that end. Even if your destination is a village, you must go forth from here after training and strive to impart sound knowledge to its people, to bring a new-world society into being, to make the Tamil Nadu of the future shine with hope, and, in short, to enable people to live as Tamils, with self-respect and the power to create a radiant future. With these words, I take my leave.”

---NOTES---
The source explicitly identifies itself as a summary of the address rather than a verbatim transcript. The English preserves that editorial status. All five OCR pages remain unchanged in the Tamil source section.

“நேரு ஆதார பயிற்சிப் பள்ளி” is rendered as “Nehru Basic Training School.” “Basic” reflects the period educational name rather than a judgment about the institution’s level.

“பண்டாரம், பாதுஷாக்கள்” juxtaposes a religious mendicant or custodian with a padishah. Both period terms are retained conservatively because the humorous contrast depends upon their social types.

The radio, Congo, Ganges, lighting, currency, puranic, and scientific examples are translated in the speaker’s argumentative voice. Their educational, political, and historical assertions have not been independently verified.
